  "query": {
    "response": {
      "query": "What are the default size limits for file uploads and total request size for the new multipart upload support on the OpenAI-compatible API endpoints?",
      "sections": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "d4a43548-8d6b-502d-8374-cc5ee3c3b4a0",
          "search_unit_id": "d4a43548-8d6b-502d-8374-cc5ee3c3b4a0",
          "block_from_id": "74637c3e-6f53-5637-8f05-b77f6d135c41",
          "block_to_id": "74637c3e-6f53-5637-8f05-b77f6d135c41",
          "section_path": "Pr 18421 Multipart File Validation Limits",
          "document_id": "cc1059a7-bd15-5bd0-8634-05538e504bc8",
          "document_version_id": "71c7ae7b-c7e0-5d4f-bf35-98f672510fe4",
          "document_name": "github__dsid_ae068ee4aa9640159427cd941bef0238.md",
          "version_no": 1,
          "page_from": 1,
          "page_to": 1,
          "anchor": "p-2",
          "heading": "Pr 18421 Multipart File Validation Limits",
          "content": "Motivation: users integrating tool/function calling and file uploads via the OpenAI-compatibility endpoints were sending a wide variety of content-types and very large multipart requests which caused inconsistent runtime behavior, high memory pressure, and unexpected acceptance of unsupported payloads. This PR introduces a focused, backwards-compatible implementation for multipart/form-data parsing on the OpenAI-compatibility path, strict Content-Type validation for tool/file inputs, and deterministic payload limits both per-file and per-request. Summary of changes: 1) Add a streaming-safe multipart parser that yields parts rather than buffering entire requests; 2) Validate Content-Type of each part (whitelist: text/*, image/*, application/json, application/octet-stream with explicit size checks); 3) Enforce configurable limits: max_file_size (default 10MiB), max_total_request_size (default 50MiB), max_parts_count (default 20). These defaults are conservative and documented; they can be overridden at tenant or route level via API config. 4) Hook validation into the OpenAI-compat layer so existing client integrations get predictable errors (400/413) with structured error bodies when inputs are rejected. 5) Add detailed unit and integration tests and end-to-end tests for streaming uploads and for the dedicated Dedicated/Private ingress paths. Backwards compatibility notes: this does not change the non-multipart JSON body semantics. Existing users who relied on accepting arbitrary large file uploads should update their tenant config or use Redwood Dedicated with higher quotas. Rollout plan: feature-flagged behind feature.enable_multipart_input (off by default); staged rollout to internal dogfood tenants, then gradual roll to 10%, 50%, 100% via config rollout. Checklist: - [x] Streaming-safe multipart parser implemented - [x] Content-Type whitelist + validation errors - [x] Per-file and per-request size enforcement + configurable defaults - [x] OpenAI-compat error mapping and structured error shapes - [x] Tests: unit, integration, e2e - [x] Docs: compatibility notes and config guide - [x] Telemetry: counters for rejected parts and throttled requests - [x] Migration & rollout plan included Testing notes: the multipart parser uses a small constant memory footprint for each active part and yields part metadata to the handler; large parts trigger a streaming drain with early termination (413 response) to avoid OOM. Integration tests exercise combination of binary and text parts, wrong content-types, and boundary cases around the configured limits. Telemetry: added metrics api.multipart.rejected_content_type, api.multipart.rejected_size, api.multipart.accepted_parts and a hist for part sizes. Implementation details: - parser: runtime/multipart/stream_parser.py - validator: api/validation/multipart_validator.py - compat layer: api/openai_compat/input_adapter.py - config: config/defaults/multipart.yaml - docs: docs/openai-compat/multipart.md - tests: tests/unit/test_multipart_parser.py, tests/integration/test_openai_compat_multipart.py Error shape examples (structured output): when a part is rejected we return 413 with a JSON body {\"error\":{\"type\":\"request_too_large\",\"message\":\"part exceeds max_file_size\",\"meta\":{\"part_name\":\"file\",\"limit_bytes\":10485760}}} and 400 for unsupported content-types with type unsupported_media_type. Observability: trace spans added around parse->validate->route dispatch to help pinpoint latency regressions. Related items: Linear ENG-2728 (multipart uploader stability), PM-146 (OpenAI compat feature parity).\nLiam Chen: key question — do we need to special-case application/pdf? many partners send PDFs as application/pdf; current whitelist would block it. Suggest adding application/pdf or making whitelist configurable per-tenant.\nAisha Patel: replied — agreed, added application/pdf to tenant_whitelist in config and made default conservative. Added test case + note in docs explaining why binary blobs should be limited.",
          "score": 0.852932,
          "rank": 0,
          "source_id": "03c7d2c1966e43be80f942e7b7119a04",
          "source_name": "Project db06a2fc373766adbe24ce04"
